= Prelude

What can be computed? This class should really be called _Computability Theory_.

Classically, we conceive of the notion of "computing" something as an operation
that could be done by a _human_ computer. A reasonable mental model is a person
equipped with an unlimited amount of scratch paper who could follow exact,
precise instructions without mistakes (e.g. given $n$ numbers, add them, then
write them in a the top left of the scratch paper), but could not parse or
execute instructions requiring "insight," e.g. "think of a proof proving
$sqrt(2)$ is irrational."

For simplicity, we can consider only methods of calculating functions on
natural numbers, $f : NN -> NN$, noting that through various abstractions we
can actually represent most problems we care about computing as functions from
$NN -> NN$.

When we ask for a "mechanical process," "effective process," or "algorithm,"
for computing a certain $f : NN -> NN$, we really mean a set of precise,
well-defined instructions for the "human computer," which when followed exactly
yields the function's value at a given input in a finite amount of steps taken.
These notions of "precise," "well-defined," and "steps" are all quite
hand-wavy, but eventually we will come up with a precise theoretical mechanism
that represents the essence of what can be done "mechanically" (and, indeed,
can be simulated by hand by a human given vast amounts of time, scratch paper,
and patience). This device is famously known as the _Turing machine_ and is
essentially the fundamental object of analysis in computation.

In the modern age, digital computers exist, which famously are able to follow
extremely precise machine instructions which we write as _code_. Modern
computers do not implement the theoretical model of a Turing machine but are
able to compute exactly the same set of functions. This is a truly miraculous
phenomenon, as it means studying the theoretical properties of incredibly
complicated modern programming languages can be reduced to studying just the
Turing machine!

We propose the extremely imprecise notion of "Python-computability." This is
not a real term in TCS.

#definition[
  A function $f : NN -> NN$ that can be computed by some Python program running
  on a computer with unlimited resources is called *Python-computable* (even if
  the runtime of the program would be so long it exceeds e.g. the heat death of
  the universe).
]

So for instance all basic arithmetic operations (thus all polynomials) are
easily Python-computable. In fact all elementary functions are
Python-computable.

#proposition[
  There are certain functions $f : NN -> NN$ which are not Python-computable.
]

#proof[
  As Python uses a finite alphabet for source code, there are only countably
  many Python programs. There are uncountably many functions $f : NN -> NN$.
]

The second fact you should have learned from a real analysis class. I learned
the first fact in my analysis course; it's a fun exercise otherwise.

*The Church-Turing Thesis*: any two reasonable notions of
computability/calculability of $f : NN -> NN$ are equivalent.

The thesis is not a formal theorem statement or anything of the sort. It's
something of a philosophical viewpoint observed by Alonzo Church and Alan
Turing after finding that every early attempt at reasonably codifying
"effective process," "computability," or "algorithm" such as Gödel's recursive
functions, Church's $lambda$-calculus, and Turing's machine, all ended up being
(provably) equivalent, i.e. they are able to solve exactly the same set of
problems (that is, compute the exact same set of $f : NN -> NN$. To challenge
the thesis, one would have to come up with a model of computation more powerful
than a Turing machine, and thus far no one has come close. (Indeed, the modern
von Neumann architecture digital computer is certainly not a challenge to the
thesis, and neither is quantum computing.)

If you believe in the Church-Turing Thesis, then it's no longer necessary to
hold the notion of Python-computability, as anything Python-computable is also
JavaScript-computable is also C-computable is also Turing-computable, so we can
just drop the prefix and ask whether things are "computable."

"Effectively calculable."

*Hilbert's 10th problem*: find a (mechanical) "process" which, given a
polynomial $p in ZZ[x_1, ..., x_n]$, $n in NN$, "determine in a finite number
of operations" whether $p$ has a root in $ZZ^n$.

#theorem[
  _MDRP_.

  No such effective process exists; Hilbert's 10th problem is decided in the negative.
]

*Hilbert's Program*: Find a set of axioms for math and an "algorithm" to
determine whether a given mathematical statement can be proven from those
axioms.

#theorem[
  _Godel, 1931_.

  This is not possible.
]

$n$-ary relations: $R subset.eq NN^n$.

#abuse[We write $R(x_0, ..., x_(n-1))$ to mean $(x_0, ..., x_(n-1)) in R$.]

Let $"Char"_R : NN^n -> NN$. Defined by
$
  "Char"_R (arrow(x)) = cases(1 "if" &R(arrow(x)), 0 "if" not &R(arrow(x))).
$

== Partial functions

#definition[
  A *partial function* from $NN -> NN$ is a function $f : D -> NN$ for some $D
  subset.eq NN$. We write $f : NN harpoon NN$.
]

For $x in NN$ we write $f(x) arrow.b$ to mean $x in D$ and $f(x) arrow.t$ to
mean $x in.not D$. "Converges," "Diverges."

#abuse[
  If $x in D and f(x) = y$ then we write $f(x) arrow.b = y$.
]

$"dom"(f) = {x | f(x) arrow.b}$, $"ran"(f) = {y(exists x in NN, f(x) arrow.b =
    y)}$.


#definition[
  If $f : NN harpoon NN and "dom"(f) = NN$ we say $f$ is *total*. Write $f : NN
  -> NN$ (like usual).
]

If $f, g : NN harpoon NN$ we say $f = g$ if
$
  forall x [(f(x) arrow.t and g(x) arrow.t) or (f(x) arrow.b and g(x) arrow.b and f(x) = g(x))].
$
(Symbol soup for they agree on convergence, and value if converges. Functional extensionality for partials.)

== Recursive partial functions

Some $n$-ary partial functions are "clearly" effectively calculable (you could
imagine a computer doing these easily).

- Composition: if we already have two effectively calculable programs
  (functions), then composing them is trivial.

- For $n in NN$, let $C_0^n : NN^n -> NN$ be $C_0^n (arrow(x)) = 0$. Let $s : NN -> NN$ be $s(x) = x+1$. ($s$ for _successor_.)

- For $k < n in NN$, let $pi^n_k : NN^n -> NN$ be the "$k$-th projection"
  $
    pi^n_k (x_0, ..., x_(n-1)) = x_k.
  $

- For $k, n in NN$, $h : NN^k harpoon NN$, $g_0, ..., g_(k-1) : NN^n harpoon NN$, let
  $
    f(arrow(x)) = h(g_0 (arrow(x)), g_1 (arrow(x)), ..., g_(k-1) (arrow(x))) : NN^n -> NN.
  $
  Here we say $f$ is *defined by substitution* from $h, g_0, ..., g_(k-1)$.

Let $cal(C)$ be the family of partial functions $NN^* -> NN$. An additional, very important rule:

- Recursion: let $h : NN -> NN$, $k in NN$. Define $f : NN -> NN$ by $f(0) =
  k$ and $forall x, f(x + 1) = h(f(x))$.

  More generally, for $n in NN$, $g : NN^n -> NN$, $h : NN^(n + 2) -> NN$,
  define $f : NN^(n+1) -> NN$. For $arrow(x) in NN^n$, take $f(arrow(x), 0) =
  g(arrow(x))$, and $forall y, f(arrow(x), y + 1) = h(arrow(x), f(arrow(x), y),
    y)$.

  Here we say that $f$ is *defined by primitive recursion* from $g$ and $h$. If
$g, h in cal(C)$, then $f in cal(C)$. Please note this is not the recursion you
know and love from your intro CS class. In particular, it _always terminates_.

#example[
  $+ : NN^2 -> NN$ is in $cal(C)$.
]

#proof[
  Use primitive recursion,
  $
                    x + 0 & = x \
    forall y, x + (y + 1) & = s(x + y) = s(pi_1^3(x, x+y, y)).
  $
]

In the proof above, explicitly speaking, we chose $g(x) = "id"_NN$. The choice
of $h$ is also clear. Here $arrow(x) in RR$, so given $f(x, y + 1) = x + (y +
  1)$, a suitable choice is $h = s compose pi_1^3$, since $h(x, x + y, y) =
s(x+y) = x
+ (y + 1) = f(x, y + 1)$.

Beyond just satisfying the symbolic constraints we attempt to give an intuitive
explanation for why this is a clear choice. We should interpret the $(n+1)$-ary
function $f$ as the familiar _for loop_. The first $n$ parameters constitute
the vector $arrow(x)$, and the $(n+1)^"th"$ argument can be seen as the loop
index $i$. We should view $arrow(x)$ as some immutable auxiliary data that the
loop can access throughout its iterations. Indeed, notice that $arrow(x)$ is
passed unchanged throughout every recursive step of $f$, $g$, and $h$.

The $n$-ary function $h$ can be interpreted as an initial value at $i = 0$, and
the $(n+2)$-ary function $h$ is the loop body. The $(n+1)^"th"$ argument of $h$
is some value passed down from the previous iteration of the loop. The way I
think about it is that at index $i$ of the loop, which is $f(arrow(x), i)$, we
can pass on some value to the next iteration $i + 1$. We can therefore access
the value passed to us by the previous iteration $i - 1$, which is why
$f(arrow(x), y + 1) = h(arrow(x), f(x, y), y)$.

Once we digest primitive recursion from the for loop perspective, it becomes
more palatable as a "primitive" form of recursion. Essentially, instead of a
recursive function (in the colloquial sense) being able to arbitrarily call
itself in its body, a primitive recursive function $f(arrow(x), y)$ can only
obtain the value of $f(arrow(x), y - 1)$ in its body, and the base case is
guaranteed to be when $y = 0$. In this view, $g$ and $h$ are merely auxiliary
functions to make the formalism work out.

Now the choice of $h = s compose pi^3_1$ is clear. For $x + y$, we just need a
for loop to add $1$ to $x$, $y$ times. We don't need the initial input $x$, and
we don't need the index $y$. So we use $pi^3_1$ to choose the prior value of
the "loop," and then we add one to it (via the successor function). The initial
value of the loop is clearly $h(x) = x$ itself. Now the function $f$ defined by
primitive recursion can be interpreted as a for loop with an accumulator
variable initialized at $x$, at each following iteration adding $1$ to the
accumulator, running for a total of $y + 1$ times. (The first iteration where
$x$ is initialized is interpreted as $x + 0$, so the loop adds one to $x$ a
total of $y$ times, computing $x + y$ in the end.)

Primitive recursion for partials: for partial functions $g : NN^n harpoon NN$,
$h : NN^(n+2) harpoon NN$, the function $f : NN^(n+1) harpoon NN$ *defined by
primitive recursion* from $g$ and $h$ is given by
$
  forall z, f(arrow(x), 0) arrow.b &= z <==> g(arrow(x)) arrow.b = z \
  forall y, f(arrow(x), y+1) arrow.b &= z <==> exists m, [f(arrow(x), y) arrow.b = m and h(arrow(x), m, y) arrow.b = z].
$

Just means that when working with partials we need to take care of convergence.

Here is the last rule, called minimization.

- Let $n in NN$, $g : NN^(n+1) -> NN$. Define $f : NN^n -> NN$ by
  $
    f(arrow(x)) arrow.b = y &<==> y "is the least natural number s.t." g(arrow(x), y) = 0 \
    &<==> g(arrow(x), y) = 0 and forall z < y, g(arrow(x), z) != 0 \
    f(arrow(x)) arrow.t &<==> forall y, g(arrow(x), y) != 0.
  $


  If $y in cal(C)$, we'll put $f in cal(C)$. Notation: $f(arrow(x)) = underbrace(mu y, "'least'" y) [g(arrow(x), y) = 0]$.

  We say that $f$ is defined from $g$ by *minimization*.

  In the partial case, for $g : NN^(n+1) harpoon NN$, define $f(arrow(x)) = mu y[g(arrow(x), y) = 0]$ by
  $
    f(arrow(x)) arrow.b = y <==> g(arrow(x), y) arrow.b = 0 and forall z < y, [g(arrow(x), z) arrow.b and g(arrow(x), z) != 0].
  $

#definition[
  Call a family $cal(C)$ of partial functions $NN^* harpoon NN$ *recursively
  closed* if it satisfies all the previous construction axioms.
]

#definition[
  $
    cal(R) = inter.big_(cal(C) "is recursively closed") cal(C).
  $
  If $f in cal(R)$, we say $f$ is *recursive*.
]

Equivalently, $f$ is recursive iff it can be defined from $s$, $C_0^n$,
$pi_k^n$, using finitely many applications of substitution, primitive
recursion, and minimization.

Non-mathematical claim: recursive functions are _effectively calculable_. If
you believe in the Church-Turing Thesis, then recursive functions are exactly
the functions which can be computed by effective methods (e.g.
Python-computation).

Note that it's not clear that this is true yet! We should at least prove the
equivalency of languages decidable by Turing machines and $cal(R)$ to convince
ourselves.

#lemma[
  The following are in $cal(R)$:

  1. $"pred" : NN -> NN$
    $
      "pred"(x) = cases(0 &"if" x = 0, x - 1 &"if" x > 0).
    $
  2. $minus.dot : NN^2 -> NN$ (truncated subtraction)
    $
      x minus.dot y = cases(0 &"if" x < y, x - y &"if" x >= y) .
    $
  3. $ceil(x/(y+1)) : NN^2 -> NN$

]

#proof[
  1. Use primitive recursion:
    $
          "pred"(0) & = 0. \
      "pred"(y + 1) & = y = pi_1^2 ("pred"(y), y).
    $
  2. Use primitive recursion:
    $
      h(x) & = x \
         g & = "pred" compose pi^3_1.
    $
    Check: $f(x, y + 1) = h(x, f(x, y), y) = "pred"(f(x, y))$. Then $f(x, y) =
    "pred"^y (x)$ and if $y >= x$ then $"pred"$ already bottoms out at 0.
  3.

]

#theorem[
  Let $f : NN^(n+1) harpoon NN$ be recursive.
  1. Define $g : NN^(n+1) harpoon NN$ by
    $
      g(arrow(x), y) arrow.b = z <==> forall i < y, f(arrow(x), i) arrow.b and z = sum_(i < y) f(arrow(x), i).
    $
    Then $g in cal(R)$.
  2. Define $h : NN^(n+1)$ by
    $
      h(arrow(x), y) arrow.b = z <==> forall i < y, f(arrow(x), i) arrow.b and z = Pi_(i < y) f(arrow(x), i).
    $
]

#proof[
  1. Use primitive recursion,
  $
    g(arrow(x), 0) = 0 \
    g(arrow(x), y + 1) = g(arrow(x), y) + f(arrow(x), y).
  $
  2. Similar.
]

== Recursive relations

#definition[
  Let $n in NN$, $R subset.eq NN^n$, $R$ is called *recursive* if $"Char"_R$ is
  recursive.
]

Note $"Char"_R$ is always total.

#theorem[
  Let $k, n in NN$. Let $R$ be a $k$-ary recursive relation and $f_0, ..., f_(k-1)$ be $n$-ary _total_ recursive functions. Define $Q subset.eq NN^n$ by $Q(arrow(x)) <==> R(f_0 (arrow(x)), ..., f_(k-1) (arrow(x)))$.

  Then $Q$ is a recursive relation.
]

#proof[
  $
    "Char"_Q (arrow(x)) = "Char"_R (f_0 (arrow(x)), ..., f_(k -1) (arrow(x))).
  $
]

#theorem[
  The recursive relations are closed under $not, and, or, ==>$.
]

#proof[
  - $"Char"_(not R) = 1 minus.dot "Char"_R (arrow(x))$.
  - $"Char"_(R and Q) (arrow(x)) = "Char"_R (arrow(x)) dot "Char"_Q (arrow(x))$.
  - $R or Q = not (not R and not Q)$.
  - $R ==> Q = not R or Q$.
]

#proposition[
  The binary relations $=, !=, >=, >, <=, <$ are recursive.
]

#proof[
  - $"Char"_= = 1 minus.dot |x - y| = 1 minus.dot [(x minus.dot y) + (y minus.dot x)]$.
  - $eq.not = not eq$.
  - $x <= y <==> x minus.dot y = 0$.
  - $> = not <=$.
  - etc.
]

#theorem[
  Let $n in NN$. $f, g : NN^n -> NN$. Recursive total $R$ recursive $n$-ary relation. Define $h : NN^n -> NN$ by
  $
    h(arrow(x)) = cases(f(arrow(x)) &"if" R(arrow(x)), g(arrow(x) &"if" not R(arrow(x)))).
  $
  Then $h$ is recursive.
]

#proof[
  $
    h(arrow(x)) = f(arrow(x)) = "Char"_R (arrow(x)) + g(arrow(x)) dot "Char"_(not R) (arrow(x)).
  $
]

#theorem[
  Let $n in NN$, $R subset.eq NN^(n+1)$. Define $f : NN^n harpoon NN$ by
  $
    f(arrow(x)) = mu y [R(arrow(x), y)].
  $

  Note $f(arrow(x)) arrow.b <==> exists y, R(arrow(x), y)$. Then $f in cal(R)$.
]


#proof[
  $f(arrow(x)) = mu y[1 minus.dot "Char"_R (arrow(x), y)) = 0]$.
]

*Bounded quantification.*

#theorem[
  Let $n in NN$, $R subset.eq NN^(n+1)$ recursive. Define $Q, P subset.eq NN^(n+1)$ by
  1. $Q(arrow(x), y) <==> exists z <= y, R(arrow(x), z)$.
  2. $P(arrow(x), y) <==> forall z <= y, P(arrow(x), z)$.

  Then $P$, $Q$ are recursive.
]

#theorem[
  The following relations are recursive.
  1. $x | y <==> exists z, x dot z = y$.
  2. $P(x) <==> x "is prime"$.
]

#proof[
  1. $x | y <==> exists z <= y (x dot z = y)$.
  2. $P(x) <==> forall z <= x, (z | x ==> (z = 1 or z = x))$.
]


